Z||000000||JohnnyK||09-27-2000||04:58 PM||||Argh... All this to fix my hard shifting problems.. I hope it IS a pilot bearing or a clutch fork. Anyways, ran into a few mild problems, but the main one was that when I was turning on of the rear cradle bolts, I noticed that the nut was turning with it inside... Soooo, instead of cutting the head of the bolt of, I cut a hole in the frame and grabbed it with vice grips. now I just have to weld that nut back in there and close that hole. I will post some pictures of my work.. Also I can easily redo the mystery weld (for those of you who do not know what I am talking about, go to <A HREF="http://members.home.net/gkrikorian" TARGET=_blank>http://members.home.net/gkrikorian</A>    I think my problem was one of a kind. I actually think putting it in will be easier, minus the electric problems...||||reg||1
Z||000001||mshill||09-28-2000||09:31 PM||||John,  isn't that a good feeling to get it out.  Let me tell you that it is even better once it is back in.  Have you considered swapping in a good cradle since you have it out?  Having done a rebuild on my V6 about six months ago, I would bet you could get everything off the old cradle and on the new in less than a week during your spare time (LOL, like any of us have a whole lot of that).  <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||JohnnyK||09-28-2000||11:07 PM||||Yeah.. I think I might do that if I can find a good cradle around here for cheap... However, that looks despairing. (Hey contra, have anything?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> I have to make new front bushings for the cradle too. But maybe not if I can find a new cradle.. You should check out the pics (Adding new ones right now)||||reg||1
